**New Evidence**
Welfare priority and children/families
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Policy Speech by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio to the 208th Session of the Diet","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-01-17","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202201/_00009.html","claim":"Kishida said responding to COVID-19 was the Kishida administration's highest priority and framed it as a government duty to protect citizens' lives and health with swift action.","locator":"lines 27-29 and 36-39","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript published the same day; direct statement by the prime minister.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous statement","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida cabinet policy statement; outcome still depends on ministries, local governments, and implementation.","contrary_evidence":["This is an intent statement, not proof that every promised welfare effect was realized in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Policy Speech by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio to the 208th Session of the Diet","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-01-17","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202201/_00009.html","claim":"Kishida tied women's economic independence, anti-violence measures, loneliness support, fertility treatment coverage, the Children and Families Agency, and disability support to his new capitalism agenda.","locator":"lines 209-223","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript with direct policy commitments and institutional design language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous statement","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida agenda-setting; downstream effects depend on legislation, ministries, and budgets.","contrary_evidence":["This is a policy vision and not a measured outcome; high inherited Japanese welfare levels limit how much marginal improvement can be inferred from rhetoric alone."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.3","6B.7","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Policy Speech by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio to the 208th Session of the Diet","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-01-17","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202201/_00009.html","claim":"Kishida said Japan would objectively evaluate its COVID responses, compile medium- and long-term measures, and strengthen the control-tower function for the next infectious-disease crisis.","locator":"lines 102-103","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript; the statement is explicit about evaluation, revision, and institutional learning.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous statement","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida cabinet policy commitment; implementation would be shared across the health bureaucracy.","contrary_evidence":["This is process-oriented evidence rather than proof of measured welfare gains in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by the Prime Minister regarding His Telephone Talk with President Zelensky of Ukraine","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202202/_00016.html","claim":"Kishida said Japan would provide another US$100 million in emergency humanitarian assistance, set up liaison support in Poland, and allow visa extensions for Ukrainians in Japan while helping evacuate Japanese nationals.","locator":"lines 20-27","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript with concrete aid, evacuation, and visa measures announced by the prime minister.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous statement","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida foreign-aid and evacuation policy announcement; humanitarian delivery involved other agencies and partners.","contrary_evidence":["The aid was aimed at a foreign crisis, so its domestic welfare value is indirect and should not be overstated."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-04-08","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202204/_00004.html","claim":"Kishida said Japan would take emergency measures to protect daily lives from energy and commodity price shocks and strengthen medical treatment and testing systems while escalating sanctions on Russia.","locator":"lines 21-25 and 38-40","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript with direct statements on household protection and crisis management.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous statement","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida crisis-response policy; the effect depends on budget execution and market conditions.","contrary_evidence":["This is crisis mitigation in an imported-shock environment, not evidence of durable structural welfare transformation."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10","6B.2"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Video Message by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio at the Stand Up For Ukraine Pledging Event for Evacuees and Internally Displaced People Convened by European Commission and Canada","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-04-09","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202204/_00007.html","claim":"Kishida pledged a total of US$300 million for Ukraine, including emergency humanitarian aid for health care, food assistance, and protection of displaced people, plus loans to support Ukraine's economy.","locator":"lines 17-19","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript with specific amounts and aid categories; directly attributable to the prime minister.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous statement","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida cabinet pledge; actual delivery would also depend on international organizations and NGOs.","contrary_evidence":["The pledge is external-facing humanitarian assistance, so it supports welfare orientation indirectly rather than measuring domestic delivery."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202206/_00006.html","claim":"Kishida said the Act to Establish the Children and Families Agency passed, fertility treatment became covered by insurance from April, and the government would raise the childbirth lump-sum allowance while opening a 300-staff preparation office.","locator":"lines 46-48","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript with concrete legislative and budgetary child-policy commitments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous statement","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida cabinet-sponsored policy and legislative agenda; downstream implementation would be shared with ministries and the Diet.","contrary_evidence":["The main implementation dates fall partly after 2022, so this is stronger on agenda-setting and institution-building than on within-year outcome proof."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Outline of the Act Establishing the Children and Families Agency (Act No. 75 of 2022)","publisher":"Children and Families Agency","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.cfa.go.jp/en/act-establishing-the-CFA-en","claim":"The 2022 act created a Cabinet Office agency tasked with child welfare, child health, parenting support, child advocacy, child-benefit functions, and child-abuse prevention, and made child-related policy coordination a central cabinet function.","locator":"purpose section and outline items 1-5","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government explanation of the enacted law; highly reliable on the institutional design and responsibilities it created.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 act; the webpage is later but describes a 2022 enacted institutional change","ruler_attribution":"Kishida cabinet legislative initiative; concrete implementation and staffing were shared with the bureaucracy.","contrary_evidence":["The agency's effectiveness cannot be inferred from the statute alone, and its full operational start was in 2023."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"不妊治療に関する取組","publisher":"Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare","publication_date":"2022-02-09","url":"https://www.mhlw.go.jp/stf/seisakunitsuite/bunya/kodomo/kodomo_kosodate/boshi-hoken/funin-01_00004.html","claim":"The MHLW said that, based on evidence-level evaluation by the Japanese Society of Reproductive Medicine, general infertility treatment and assisted reproductive medicine became newly covered by insurance from April 2022.","locator":"lines 280-284","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministry page describing the evidence-based policy basis and coverage change.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous administrative record","ruler_attribution":"Kishida cabinet health-policy action, but the technical evidence review and implementation were carried out by the ministry and expert bodies.","contrary_evidence":["Insurance coverage alone does not prove equitable access or measured fertility outcomes by year-end 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"令和4年6月に成立した改正児童福祉法について","publisher":"Children and Families Agency","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://www.cfa.go.jp/policies/jidougyakutai/Revised-Child-Welfare-Act","claim":"The June 2022 revised Child Welfare Act responded to child abuse and isolated caregiving conditions by adding municipal comprehensive support, child-family centers, and a certified qualification in child-welfare practice.","locator":"official page summary / search snippet describing the 2022 revision","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government summary of a 2022 law; the page is later but the law and policy change are clearly described.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 law and 2022 policy context; later webpage summarizes the change","ruler_attribution":"Kishida cabinet legislative agenda with municipal implementation; outcomes remain partly downstream.","contrary_evidence":["This shows institutional reform, not direct proof that abuse or isolation fell in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Opening Remarks by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio at the World Assembly for Women: WAW! 2022","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-12-03","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202212/_00002.html","claim":"Kishida said women's economic independence was central to the new form of capitalism, that Japan would close the gender wage gap by mandatory disclosure, promote post-delivery paternity leave, and provide temporary shelters for women fleeing Afghanistan and Ukraine.","locator":"lines 45-51","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript with direct gender-equality and vulnerable-group commitments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous statement","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida policy framing and external-aid commitment; implementation involved multiple ministries and partners.","contrary_evidence":["These are commitments and aid examples, not a direct measure of women’s lived outcomes in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.7","6B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-12-10","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202212/_00004.html","claim":"Kishida said the government would provide average households with 47,000 yen of assistance, give 100,000 yen in support to pregnant women and families raising children without income caps, raise the childbirth lump-sum allowance to 500,000 yen, and keep children at the center of policy.","locator":"lines 44-53","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript with concrete, quantified support measures and explicit universality language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous statement","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida cabinet budget and family-policy announcement; execution depended on the Diet and administrative agencies.","contrary_evidence":["The package was announced during high inflation, so some relief may only offset losses rather than create net welfare gains."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-12-10","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202212/_00004.html","claim":"Kishida said the supplementary budget would put 1 trillion yen over five years into people through reskilling, career changes, and transitions to permanent employment, and that his task force would check the progress of all budget measures every week.","locator":"lines 55-63","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript; the monitoring mechanism and workforce-investment scale are explicit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-period 2022, direct contemporaneous statement","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida cabinet management choice; actual take-up and labor-market effects depended on firms, workers, and ministries.","contrary_evidence":["This is strong on monitoring and planned investment, but weak on verified end-year welfare outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
Context and outcomes
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Japan","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"unknown (live dashboard; latest year 2022)","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/jpn/","claim":"Japan's latest 2022 child-related SDG figures show near-universal water and sanitation access, 99 percent DTP3 coverage, 98 percent MCV1 coverage, and very low maternal and under-five mortality.","locator":"dashboard rows for water, sanitation, immunization, and mortality indicators","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UNICEF dashboard using international agency data; strong for country-level outcome context, though not ruler-specific.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"latest available year 2022, useful baseline context for the target year","ruler_attribution":"Country-level baseline only; does not isolate Kishida's marginal contribution.","contrary_evidence":["High national averages can hide subgroup gaps, and the dashboard does not attribute changes to the prime minister."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PISA 2022 Results (Volume I and II) - Country Notes: Japan","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2023-12-05","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/pisa-2022-results-volume-i-and-ii-country-notes_ed6fbcc5-en/japan_f7d7daad-en.html","claim":"OECD reported that Japan remained among the highest-performing systems in PISA 2022, with improved student belonging since 2018, but still experienced long COVID-related school closures for 16 percent of students and weekly remote-learning difficulties for some students.","locator":"country note sections on performance, school life, and learning during COVID-related school closures","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official OECD country note with standard PISA methodology and clear 2022 Japan indicators.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 educational outcomes and disruptions, published after the test but directly about the target period","ruler_attribution":"Country-system context only; useful for baseline and crisis-education effects, not direct prime-minister attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The note does not isolate Kishida's specific causal impact, and much of the educational baseline long predates 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Basic statistics of Japan, 2022: OECD Economic Surveys: Japan 2024","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2024","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2024/01/oecd-economic-surveys-japan-2024_9289b572/full-report/component-2.html","claim":"OECD's basic-statistics table for Japan reported a 15.7 percent relative poverty rate, 9.9 percent female share of parliament, 2.6 percent of GNI spent on education, and 11.5 percent spent on health care.","locator":"society / public and private spending rows in the basic statistics table","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official OECD table compiled from recognized international datasets; strong for baseline and residual-gap context.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"published after the target year but reporting baseline context from 2018-2021 alongside 2022 survey framing","ruler_attribution":"Country-level baseline and inherited constraints only; not direct evidence of Kishida-specific change.","contrary_evidence":["The table mostly reflects inherited structural conditions and does not prove 2022 ruler-specific improvement or deterioration."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across PMO speeches, MHLW and Children and Families Agency pages, UNICEF, and OECD. I kept the strongest Japan-specific, contemporaneous or near-contemporaneous claims and dropped near-duplicates that repeated the same child-policy or Ukraine announcements in different wrappers.

Rejected or de-emphasized items:
- The PMO child-support overview page at `childsupport.html?os=i` was cache-missed on open, so I did not rely on it.
- Several 2021 PMO COVID or support statements were treated as pre-period background only and not used as core 2022 evidence.
- Generic global child-poverty papers and non-Japan-specific UNICEF/OECD material were rejected as too broad for ruler attribution.
- Reuters/secondary writeups repeating PMO announcements were mostly dropped in favor of the primary Japanese transcripts.
- Broader OECD demographic-headwind summaries were not used when the Japan 2022 country note and basic-statistics table already covered the relevant baseline and residual gaps.
- I kept both positive and limiting evidence, but I did not pad the dossier with multiple sources describing the same policy event in slightly different language.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct 2022 evidence on whether Kishida's child/fertility package actually changed child poverty, birth outcomes, or service uptake by year-end is still thin.
- More Japan-specific 2022 outcome data on disabled children, single-parent households, housing insecurity, and food insecurity would improve attribution.
- I still lack a strong independent 2022 evaluation of the distributional impact of the universal cash relief and child-support package.
- The evidence is strongest on agenda-setting, legislation, and crisis relief, and weaker on measured end-of-year life-chance gains.

I did not score.
